---
// src/components/ComputerIslandSection.astro
//
// Same family as ArtIslandSection.astro: two flanking illustrations
// sitting behind centered-ish text, one JS-driven mouse-repel script,
// one CSS bob loop per island. Layout differs though — per the
// reference screenshot, neither island bleeds off the section's edges
// here (both sit fully inside the frame), and the arrangement is
// left-middle / top-right rather than top-left / bottom-right:
//
//   - `leftImage`  (telephone-pole island, svg)  -> mid-left, roughly
//     vertically centered
//   - `rightImage` (computer island, gif)        -> upper-right,
//     larger
//
// Because nothing bleeds off an edge here, offsets are plain vw/vh
// instead of the clamp(mobileValue, calc(a + b*vw), desktopValue)
// trick used in ArtIslandSection. That trick existed there to bridge
// a fixed *rem* offset (which doesn't scale with viewport width) up to
// a *vw*-scaled width. Since every offset below is already vw/vh, it
// scales continuously with the viewport on its own — no separate
// mobile breakpoint needed for position. If it still looks cramped on
// very narrow phones (the two width clamps have a px floor, so an
// island can stop shrinking before its offset does), add a
// `max-width: 640px` query the same way CornerIsland.astro does, or
// just ask to have it tuned.
//
// NOTE on rightImage: the gif you're using is ~19MB, which is a lot
// for a background decoration — worth converting to a compressed
// .webm/.mp4 (via <video autoplay loop muted playsinline>) or a
// lighter-weight animated webp if you get a chance, since a 19MB file
// will noticeably slow down first load, especially on mobile. Left it
// as a plain <img> with loading="lazy" for now so it doesn't block
// anything above it, but that doesn't shrink the download itself.
//
// Background: originally a real image (`backgroundImage` prop), via
// `background-image` + `background-size: cover`, `min-height: 100vh`
// — same technique every other section in this family uses. The
// goal, per your clarification: every section keeps a CONSISTENT
// height across every screen size, with `cover` cropping whichever
// dimension needs it least to make that happen with no gaps. Briefly
// tried sizing by height only (`auto 100%`, cropping only the sides)
// per your request to stop shortening backgrounds vertically on wide
// screens, but that left visible white space on screens wider (in
// aspect ratio) than the image itself, which you didn't want either —
// back to `cover`.
//
// New: swapped that background image for a plain CSS gradient
// (`linear-gradient`, see `.computer-section` below), matching a
// reference photo you sent — a deep-blue-to-cyan vertical sky gradient.
// The `backgroundImage` prop is gone entirely (removed from `Props`,
// the destructuring, the `<section>`'s inline style, and this file's
// own `Usage` example below) rather than left unused, since nothing
// in this section needs an image file any more. This also retires the
// long-standing "I still don't have the actual background image file"
// caveat that used to sit lower in this comment — there's no image to
// not-have any more.
//
// One more layer on top of that: on a short/wide viewport, `cover`
// still has to crop SOMETHING to avoid a gap, and per your follow-up
// that "something" shouldn't ever be the image's own height either —
// you'd rather scroll than see it shortened. Fixed by adding
// `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` (the image's real ratio) alongside
// `min-height`/`width: 100%` on `.computer-section` below, so the
// section itself grows taller than one screen on those viewports
// instead of asking `cover` to crop into the height — see the comment
// there.
//
// No seam-fade classes any more — the shared fade-band system that
// used to blend section edges together was removed per your request,
// so this section now just butts directly against ArtIslandSection
// above it and EducationIslandSection below it.
//
// Still carries `.sky-chain`, so the gap-collapsing on both sides
// keeps working regardless of what backs it.
//
// Now also carries `.halftone-hover`, joining the single, page-wide
// cursor-spotlight element in Layout.astro (`.halftone-spotlight`,
// see the comment there) purely by having this class — no markup or
// script needed here for it, since `body:has(.halftone-hover:hover)
// .halftone-spotlight` is what actually turns the spotlight on.
//
// New: `.computer-section` now has an explicit `z-index: -1;` (below)
// — not for anything in THIS file, but because ArtIslandSection's
// moon layer now deliberately bleeds down past its own section's
// bottom edge into this one, and this section's own full-bleed
// background image was winning a DOM-order stacking tie and painting
// over it. See the comment on `.computer-section` itself for the full
// reasoning, and ArtIslandSection.astro's own top-of-file comment for
// the moon-bleed side of this.
//
// Usage:
//   <ComputerIslandSection
//     id="section-cs"
//     eyebrow="Island 02"
//     title="Lorem Ipsum Dolor"
//     text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet..."
//     leftImage="/images/telephone_pole_island.svg"
//     rightImage="/images/computer_island.gif"
//     cloudsBackground="/images/computer_island_background_ver2.webp"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  leftImage: string;
  rightImage: string;
  cloudsBackground: string;
}

const { id, eyebrow, title, text, leftImage, rightImage, cloudsBackground } = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="computer-section sky-chain halftone-hover"
  id={id}
>
  <div class="computer-clouds-bg" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="computer-clouds-track">
      <img class="computer-clouds-tile" src={cloudsBackground} alt="" />
      <img class="computer-clouds-tile" src={cloudsBackground} alt="" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="computer-island computer-island-left" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="computer-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <img src={leftImage} alt="" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    <p class="body-text">{text}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="computer-island computer-island-right" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="computer-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <img src={rightImage} alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-repel drift as ArtIslandSection.astro, driving every
  // `.parallax-inner` element on the page. If both components end up
  // on the same page, each ships its own copy of this script and each
  // one queries ALL `.parallax-inner` elements globally (not just its
  // own) — that's intentional/harmless (both compute the same easing
  // toward the same targets), just a little redundant. Worth hoisting
  // into one shared script later if a third section joins this family.
  const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
    "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
  ).matches;

  if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
    const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
    const ease = 0.08;

    let mouseX = 0;
    let mouseY = 0;
    let hasMouse = false;

    const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
      el,
      currentX: 0,
      currentY: 0,
    }));

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      mouseX = e.clientX;
      mouseY = e.clientY;
      hasMouse = true;
    });

    function tick() {
      for (const s of state) {
        let targetX = 0;
        let targetY = 0;

        if (hasMouse) {
          const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
          const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
          const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

          const dx = restX - mouseX;
          const dy = restY - mouseY;
          const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

          if (dist < influenceRadius) {
            const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
            const nx = dx / dist;
            const ny = dy / dist;
            targetX = nx * maxOffset * falloff;
            targetY = ny * maxOffset * falloff;
          }
        }

        s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
        s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
        s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
      }
      requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }
</script>

<style>
  .computer-section {
    position: relative;
    /* Explicit (not the default `auto`) so this section establishes
       its OWN local stacking context — needed so ArtIslandSection's
       moon (which now deliberately bleeds down past ITS OWN section's
       bottom edge, per your request) can actually show through here
       instead of being hidden behind this section's own full-bleed
       background image. Without this, `.computer-section` (position:
       relative, z-index left at auto) and `.art-section` (explicit
       z-index: 0, see its own comment) painted at the SAME stacking
       tier, and same-tier ties break by DOM order — this section
       comes AFTER Art in the markup, so it was winning that tie and
       painting its own background image on top of Art's bleeding moon
       every time, which is exactly why the moon looked "cut off" right
       at the seam instead of fading out naturally past it. `z-index:
       -1` makes the comparison unambiguous (`0 > -1`) instead of a
       DOM-order tie, so Art's section now reliably wins in their
       overlap zone. This section's OWN foreground content (the two
       islands at z-index: 1, the text at z-index: 2, below) is
       unaffected — those z-index values are now scoped locally to
       this section's new stacking context, so they still correctly
       paint in front of this section's own background, exactly as
       before. */
    z-index: -1;
    min-height: 100vh;
    /* `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` used to matter for matching the old
       background image's own real dimensions (3840x2160) so `cover`
       never had to crop it top/bottom — with the image gone in favor
       of a gradient (see the top-of-file comment), there's nothing
       left to protect from cropping, but this section's overall SHAPE
       (how tall it grows on unusual viewports) is a separate concern
       from what paints inside it, and changing it wasn't asked for,
       so it's left in place as a plain fixed target ratio: combined
       with `width: 100%` and `min-height`, the browser uses whichever
       constraint needs MORE height — on a viewport wider-than-16:9
       (mainly short/wide windows), the aspect-ratio wins and this
       section grows taller than one screen; on a taller-than-16:9
       viewport (e.g. mobile portrait), `min-height: 100vh` wins
       instead, so the section stays exactly one screen tall. `width:
       100%` is required alongside `aspect-ratio` — without it, on the
       min-height-wins branch the browser would stretch this section's
       WIDTH to satisfy the ratio instead of its height, overflowing
       past the viewport edges. */
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `overflow: hidden` here — see the comment on `overflow-x:
       hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal
       clipping page-wide, so an island that needs more vertical room
       than this section provides isn't hard-clipped. */
    /* Swapped from an image (`backgroundImage` prop, now removed)
       to a plain CSS gradient, matching the reference photo you sent
       — colors sampled directly from that file (top-to-bottom bands
       at 0/15/30/45/60/75/90/100% height): a deep blue at the top
       easing into a bright cyan that's mostly settled in by the
       60-70% mark and stays flat the rest of the way down, which is
       why the stops below cluster earlier (0/25/50%) and spread out
       later (75/100%) rather than sitting at even intervals — an
       evenly-spaced gradient would wash out that "settles into cyan
       early, stays cyan" shape the photo actually has. Plain gradients
       don't need `cover`/`position`/`repeat` (nothing to crop or
       tile), so those three rules are gone along with the image. */
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      #2d50ea 0%,
      #2e76ed 25%,
      #CFD7FC 50%,
      #38d5fa 75%,
      #1adef9 100%
    );
  }

  /* Looping clouds layer, sitting on top of the gradient (the
     gradient is this section's own `background-image`, which always
     paints furthest back regardless of any child's z-index — no
     negative z-index needed here to keep it behind, unlike
     ArtIslandSection's moon/clouds pair, which had each other AND a
     background image to stack against). `z-index: 0` just keeps this
     explicit and in front of the plain gradient, behind the
     foreground islands (`z-index: 1`) and text (`z-index: 2`) below.
     Same two-tile `translateX` loop as ArtIslandSection's
     `.art-clouds-bg` / InBetweenSection2's `.in-between-2-clouds-bg`
     — see either file's top-of-file comment for the full mechanics
     (real `<img>` tiles sized by `height: 100%; width: auto` rather
     than `background-image` + `cover`, animated `transform:
     translateX` from -50% to 0% on a `width: max-content` track).
     `computer_island_background_ver2.webp` (3840x2160, RGBA) had the
     same kind of seam problem `in_between_2_clouds_background.webp`
     needed fixing for earlier — its own left and right edges didn't
     match (measured ~27.5 average, ~249 max per-channel difference) —
     so it went through the identical roll-to-center +
     mirrored-cross-fade reprocessing before being wired up here; the
     edge mismatch is now ~0.27 average, ~0.8 max. `overflow: hidden`
     clips the track (wider than the section, holding two full tiles)
     down to the section's own bounds. */
  .computer-clouds-bg {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .computer-clouds-track {
    display: flex;
    width: max-content;
    height: 100%;
    /* Back to animated drift. Briefly set to `none` right after this
       first went in, on a request to stop "the background gradient"
       from moving — turned out that was about this clouds layer, not
       the CSS gradient underneath (which was never animated to begin
       with, so it needed no change either time); you clarified you
       do want `computer_island_background_ver2.webp` itself drifting,
       so this is back to the same two-tile `translateX` loop as
       ArtIslandSection's `.art-clouds-bg` / InBetweenSection2's
       `.in-between-2-clouds-bg`. */
    animation: computer-clouds-drift 90s linear infinite;
  }

  .computer-clouds-tile {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    width: auto;
    max-width: none;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  @keyframes computer-clouds-drift {
    from {
      transform: translateX(-50%);
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(0%);
    }
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;

    text-align: left;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .body-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .computer-island {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .computer-island-inner img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* Telephone-pole island: smaller, mid-left, roughly vertically
     centered. Doesn't bleed off any edge. */
  .computer-island-left {
    top: 52vh;
    left: 20vw;
    width: clamp(200px, 22vw, 320px);
    animation: bob-left 6.5s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  /* Computer island: larger, upper-right, its trailing cable dipping
     down toward mid-frame. Also doesn't bleed off an edge. */
  .computer-island-right {
    top: 24vh;
    right: 12vw;
    width: clamp(260px, 32vw, 440px);
    animation: bob-right 5.8s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes bob-left {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-10px); }
  }

  @keyframes bob-right {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-8px); }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .computer-island-left,
    .computer-island-right {
      animation: none;
    }

    .computer-clouds-track {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content {
      width: min(32ch, 100%);
      padding-inline: 1.5rem;
    }

    /* On mobile the powerline island's mid-left absolute position sits
       right under the (now full-width) text, so instead of overlaying
       it we drop it into normal document flow below the content. Since
       it's DOM-first (before .content), `order` is what actually moves
       it below the text visually — `.computer-section` still has
       `place-items: center`, which centers any normal-flow grid item,
       so no extra centering rule is needed once it's out of `absolute`
       positioning. The right-hand computer island is untouched; it
       wasn't reported as a problem. */
    .computer-island-left {
      position: static;
      order: 1;
      top: auto;
      left: auto;
      width: clamp(180px, 50vw, 260px);
      margin: 2.5rem auto 0;
    }

    /* `right: 12vw` (the desktop value) leaves the computer island
       close enough to center that it overlaps the now full-width text
       on narrow screens, hence the tighter `right` below. */
    .computer-island-right {
      right: -8vw;
      top: 4vh;
    }
  }
</style>